 "literal": "Suddenly right-then outwardly they-will-not-fume; time having-watched, inwardly they-will-fume, the-wise.",
 "translation": "The wise will not suddenly show their anger on the outside right away; waiting for the right time, they will keep their anger burning inside.",
 "modern": "அறிவுடையவர்கள் தமக்கு ஒரு தீங்கு நேர்ந்த உடனே வெளியே தெரியும்படி கோபப்பட மாட்டார்கள்; தக்க காலம் வரும்வரை கோபத்தை மனத்திற்குள்ளேயே அடக்கி வைத்திருப்பார்கள்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet explains how wise people handle provocation. The word 'வேர்த்தல்' literally means to sweat, which is used here as a metaphor for fuming with anger. Instead of bursting out ('பொள்ளென') and showing their anger externally right away, the wise keep their anger burning inside. They wait patiently for the right time to act.",
